## What you should build first

Don't create every file immediately. Develop in this order:

| Phase         | Deliverable                                           |
| ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| 1. Foundation | Repository, PRD, dataset, dependencies                |
| 2. Analytics  | Data cleaning, EDA, feature engineering               |
| 3. ML         | Baseline, model comparison, chronological validation  |
| 4. Dashboard  | Demand overview, 24-hour forecast, performance charts |
| 5. API        | FastAPI forecast endpoint and input validation        |
| 6. Quality    | Tests, GitHub Actions, README                         |
| 7. Deployment | Public demo and reproducible setup                    |

### Three important design decisions

- Keep notebooks for exploration, but put reusable production code in `src/voltwise/`.
- Never commit large datasets, secrets, or unnecessary model binaries to Git. Document how to obtain the data and generate artifacts.
- Prevent forecasting leakage: all lag and rolling features must use only information available at the forecast origin. Use chronological evaluation rather than random train/test splitting.

My recommendation is to begin with a working ML pipeline and Streamlit dashboard. Add FastAPI and deployment after the forecasts are reliable. That way, Voltwise will have a strong predictive analytics foundation before you invest time in the more advanced engineering features.
